<setup>
  <selectFromSequence name="k" from="2" to="6" step="1"/>
  <function name="F"> 1/(2*$k)*ln(1+$k*x^2) </function>
  <function name="F2"> 1/(2*$k)*ln|1+$k*x^2| </function>
  <derivative name="test" simplify="full">$F - $antiD</derivative>
  <derivative name="test2" simplify="full">$F2 - $antiD</derivative>

  <conditionalContent name='nextStep'
            condition="$correctFnoInt.creditAchieved = 1">
            <math name="math_limit" extend="$FnoInt.value"/>
          </conditionalContent>
</setup>

<p>
  Consider the improper integral
  <me>
    \int_0^\infty \frac{x}{1+$k x^2} \, dx
  </me>
  Complete the following sequence of prompts to explain why this improper integral diverges.
</p>

<p>
  <ol marker="a.">
    <li>
      <p>
        First, the integral is improper because <answer><choiceInput inline name="a2">
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>the interval is bounded and the integrand is continuous on the interval</choice>
          <choice credit="1">the interval is unbounded</choice>
          <choice>the integrand is unbounded at the left endpoint</choice>
          <choice>the integrand is unbounded at the right endpoint</choice>
          <choice>the integrand is unbounded at a point between the endpoints</choice>
        </choiceInput></answer>

        and so we instead consider
        <me>
          \lim_{b\to \infty} \int_0^b \frac{x}{1+$k x^2} \, dx.
        </me>

      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle  \lim_{b\to \infty} \int_0^b \frac{x}{1+$k x^2} \, dx =  \lim_{b\to \infty}\Bigg(</m><mathInput minWidth="100" name="antiD"/><m>\displaystyle\Bigg|_0^b\Bigg)</m><answer forceSmallCheckWorkButton><award><when>$antiD = $F or $test=0 or $antiD = $F2 or $test2=0</when></award></answer>
      </p>
        <!-- <boolean>$test =0 </boolean><boolean>$antiD=$F</boolean> -->
      <p>
        <m>\hspace{1.7in} = \displaystyle \lim_{b\to \infty}\Big(</m><mathInput name="FnoInt" minWidth="75"/><m>\Big)</m><answer forceSmallCheckWorkButton name="correctFnoInt"><award><when>$FnoInt = $$F(b) or $FnoInt = $$F2(b)</when></award></answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>


      <p>
        Finally, we take the limit as <m>b</m> increases without bound to determine whether the improper integral converges or diverges.  We observe that
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>
          \displaystyle \lim_{b \to \infty} \int_0^b \frac{x}{1+$k x^2} \, dx = \lim_{b \to \infty}
        </m>


        <mathInput disabled name="limitReady" prefill="$nextStep.math_limit"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        and since <m>b \to \infty</m>, it follows <m>(1+$k b^2)</m> will <choiceInput name="c1" inline>
        <choice>?</choice>
        <choice>approach <m>0</m></choice>
        <choice>approach <m>1</m></choice>
        <choice credit="1">increase without bound</choice></choiceInput>, and thus <m>\ln(1+$k b^2)</m> will <choiceInput name="c2" inline>
        <choice>?</choice>
        <choice>approach <m>0</m></choice>
        <choice>approach <m>1</m></choice>
        <choice credit="1">increase without bound</choice>
        </choiceInput>, which implies that the improper integral <m>\displaystyle \lim_{b \to \infty} \int_0^b \frac{x}{1+$k x^2} \, dx</m> <choiceInput name="c3" inline>
        <choice>?</choice>
        <choice>converges</choice>
        <choice credit="1">diverges</choice>
        </choiceInput>
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer><award><when>$c1.selectedIndices=4 and $c2.selectedIndices=4 and $c3.selectedIndices=3</when></award></answer>
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</p>
